For a counterclockwise rotation by an angle \(\theta\) in \(\mathbb{R}^2\), the matrix is

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

A \(45^\circ\) clockwise rotation is the same as a \(-45^\circ\) counterclockwise rotation, so use \(\theta = -45^\circ = -\frac{\pi}{4}\).

\[
\cos\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \qquad
\sin\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}.
\]

Thus the rotation matrix is

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(-45^\circ) & -\sin(-45^\circ) \\
\sin(-45^\circ) & \cos(-45^\circ)
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

